    },
    {
      "calls": [
        {
          "ID": "toolu_01Mv8ztewgMNoGeBPApohQqi",
          "Name": "submit_read",
          "Input": {
            "assessments": [
              {
                "assessments": [],
                "ref": "N1",
                "spans": [
                  "P17",
                  "P18",
                  "P19",
                  "P20",
                  "P142",
                  "P143",
                  "P28",
                  "P30"
                ],
                "statement": "Within five days the UK went from welcoming President Trump's reciprocal energy ceasefire, which Ukraine pledged to implement once a start date was confirmed, to saying that Russia had no interest in peace and pressing partners for sanctions and an LNG ban to make Russia heed it; the Record holds no confirmed start date.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "assessments": [
                  "N1"
                ],
                "ref": "N2",
                "spans": [
                  "P1",
                  "P2",
                  "P24",
                  "P27",
                  "P14"
                ],
                "statement": "The UK's answer to the stalled ceasefire leans on economic pressure and speeding up existing deliveries rather than new money: the £10 million brought forward is part of a £45 million package that sits beside £1.6 billion already committed, and the further packages are still being drawn up.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "assessments": [],
                "ref": "N3",
                "spans": [
                  "P111",
                  "P112",
                  "P113",
                  "P99",
                  "P100",
                  "P127",
                  "P128",
                  "P74",
                  "P65"
                ],
                "statement": "Burnham is turning his Greater Manchester record into national policy: public control of transport, procurement that favours British manufacturing and localised employment support now appear in the publicly owned railway, the English devolution agenda and the offer to Scotland.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "assessments": [
                  "N3"
                ],
                "ref": "N4",
                "spans": [
                  "P58",
                  "P61",
                  "P63",
                  "P132",
                  "P129",
                  "P62"
                ],
                "statement": "The Scotland offer is pitched against Holyrood's centralisation as much as Westminster's, with the UK Government proposing to work on regional empowerment through No10 North and the Scotland Office, and the National Economic Council meeting with the devolved First Ministers due in early November is its first test.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "assessments": [],
                "ref": "N5",
                "spans": [
                  "P46",
                  "P50",
                  "P55",
                  "P56",
                  "P57",
                  "P109",
                  "P110"
                ],
                "statement": "Burnham presents the 20 November UK-EU summit as a first step rather than an end point, selling its food and drink deal to Scottish farming and proposing a dialogue afterwards about the longer-term relationship.",
                "supersedes": ""
              }
            ],
            "statements": [
              {
                "cites": [
                  "L1"
                ],
                "stories": [],
                "text": "This week's Briefings gathered the opening moves of a Prime Minister about three months into the job: Andy Burnham has held the office since 20 July 2026."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "P17",
                  "P18",
                  "P19",
                  "P20",
                  "N1"
                ],
                "stories": [
                  {
                    "span": "P17",
                    "story": []
                  },
                  {
                    "span": "P19",
                    "story": []
                  }
                ],
                "text": "On Ukraine, the documents show the UK's position hardening within days. On 12 October Burnham welcomed President Trump's announcement of a reciprocal energy ceasefire and said President Zelenskyy had assured him Ukraine would implement it once a start date was confirmed. By 14 October the two leaders were pointing to Russian attacks since Trump's call and saying it was clear Russia had no interest in peace."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "P143",
                  "P142",
                  "P30",
                  "N1"
                ],
                "stories": [],
                "text": "London now frames its sanctions push, including the LNG maritime services ban that the Chancellor urged partners to back, as pressure on Putin to respect the US call. Nothing in the Record shows that a start date was ever confirmed."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "P24",
                  "P1",
                  "P2",
                  "N2"
                ],
                "stories": [
                  {
                    "span": "P1",
                    "story": [
                      "P138",
                      "P21"
                    ]
                  }
                ],
                "text": "The help that is actually concrete is modest: £10 million of a £45 million missile package is being brought forward, alongside £1.6 billion already committed, while the further sanctions, military and fiscal packages are still being drawn up."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "P111",
                  "P112",
                  "P113",
                  "P74",
                  "P127",
                  "P128",
                  "N3"
                ],
                "stories": [],
                "text": "At home, a pattern stands out across the stories. Burnham pointed to his record as Mayor of Greater Manchester, where he took buses back under public control and had them built in Falkirk, and said he wants all UK public procurement to work that way. The same approach runs through the plan for a publicly owned railway, with its headquarters now sited in Derby, and through his offer of devolved employment support to Scotland."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "P61",
                  "P63",
                  "P129",
                  "N4"
                ],
                "stories": [
                  {
                    "span": "P62",
                    "story": [
                      "P136",
                      "P137"
                    ]
                  }
                ],
                "text": "The Scotland offer has an edge. It casts Scotland as one of Europe's most centralised nations and proposes working through No10 North and the Scotland Office. Its first test is the National Economic Council meeting with the devolved First Ministers in early November."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "P56",
                  "P109",
                  "P110",
                  "N5"
                ],
                "stories": [],
                "text": "The 20 November UK-EU summit links the two halves of the week. Burnham sold its food and drink deal in Scotland, and he described it as a first step, to be followed by a serious dialogue about the longer-term relationship."
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
